\begin_layout Title
Econometrics II: Homework 5
\end_layout

\begin_layout Author
Nathan Smooha
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Problem 15.6
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Consider taking a large random sample of workers at a given point in time.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{\emph{sick}}_{i}=1$
\end_inset

 if person 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 called in sick during the last 90 days, and zero otherwise.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 be a vector of individual and employer characteristics.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{\emph{cigs}}_{i}$
\end_inset

 be the number of cigarettes individual 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 smokes per day (on average).
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Explain the underlying experiment of interest when we want to examine the
 effects of cigarette smoking on workdays lost.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Why might 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{\emph{cigs}}_{i}$
\end_inset

 be correlated with unobservables affecting 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{\emph{sick}}_{i}$
\end_inset

?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
One way to write the model of interest is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P(\mbox{\emph{sick}}_{i}=1|z_{i},\mbox{\emph{cigs}}_{i},q_{i})=\Phi(z_{1i}\delta_{1}+\gamma_{1}\mbox{\emph{cigs}}_{i}+q_{i})
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{1i}$
\end_inset

 is a subset of 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset

 is an unobservable variable that is possibly correlated with 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{\emph{cigs}}$
\end_inset

.
 What happens if 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset

 is ignored and you estimate the probit of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{\emph{sick}}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{1i}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{\emph{cigs}}$
\end_inset

?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Can 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{\emph{cigs}}$
\end_inset

 have a conditional normal distribution in the population? Explain.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Explain how to test whether 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{\emph{cigs}}$
\end_inset

 is exogenous.
 Does this test rely on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{\emph{cigs}}$
\end_inset

 having a conditional normal distribution?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Suppose that some of the workers live in states that recently implemented
 no-smoking laws in the workplace.
 Does the presence of the new laws suggest a good IV candidate for 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{\emph{cigs}}$
\end_inset

?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Solution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part A.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

The underlying experiment of interest is how the average amount of cigarettes
 individual 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 smokes per day affects the probability of person 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 calling in sick during the last 90 days, ceteris paribus.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part B.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Smoking is correlated with the strength of a person's immune system, which
 is an unobservable characteristic that has an affect on whether or not
 the individual will get sick.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part C.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

We can write the latent variable model as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\mbox{\emph{sick}}_{i}^{*} & = & z_{1i}^{\prime}\delta_{1}+\gamma_{1}\mbox{\emph{cigs}}_{i}+q_{i}+\epsilon_{i}\\
\mbox{\emph{sick}}_{i} & = & 1\ \left[\mbox{\emph{sick}}_{i}^{*}>0\right]
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

Because we are dealing with a probit model, we assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon_{i}\sim N(0,1)$
\end_inset

.
 Now, consider the linear projection of 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{\emph{cigs}}_{i}$
\end_inset

:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
q_{i}=\beta_{1}\mbox{\ensuremath{cigs}}_{i}+v_{i}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

 is independent of 
\bar under

\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon_{i}$
\end_inset


\bar default
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

, and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
v_{i}|z_{i},\mbox{\emph{cigs}}_{i}\sim N(0,\eta^{2})
\]

\end_inset

So, we can rewrite the latent variable as 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\mbox{\emph{sick}}_{i}^{*} & = & z_{1i}^{\prime}\delta_{1}+\gamma_{1}\mbox{\emph{cigs}}_{i}+\beta_{1}\mbox{\emph{cigs}}_{i}+v_{i}+\epsilon_{i}\\
\mbox{\emph{sick}}_{i}^{*} & = & z_{1i}^{\prime}\delta_{1}+(\gamma_{1}+\beta_{1})\mbox{\emph{cigs}}_{i}+u_{i}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}\equiv v_{i}+\epsilon_{i}$
\end_inset

 and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u_{i}|z_{i},\mbox{\emph{cigs}}_{i}\sim N(0,1+\eta^{2})
\]

\end_inset

The probit of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{\emph{sick}}_{i}^{*}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{1i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{\emph{cigs}}_{i}$
\end_inset

 is
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
P(\mbox{\emph{sick}}_{i}=1|z_{i},\mbox{\emph{cigs}}_{i}) & = & P(\mbox{\emph{sick}}_{i}^{*}>0|z_{i},\mbox{\emph{cigs}}_{i})\\
 & = & P(z_{1i}^{\prime}\delta_{1}+(\gamma_{1}+\beta_{1})\mbox{\emph{cigs}}_{i}+u_{i}>0|z_{i},\mbox{\emph{cigs}}_{i})\\
 & = & P(u_{i}>-z_{1i}^{\prime}\delta_{1}-(\gamma_{1}+\beta_{1})\mbox{\emph{cigs}}_{i}|z_{i},\mbox{\emph{cigs}}_{i},q_{i})\\
 & = & P\left(\left.\frac{u_{i}}{\sqrt{1+\eta^{2}}}<\frac{z_{1i}^{\prime}\delta_{1}+(\gamma_{1}+\beta_{1})\mbox{\emph{cigs}}_{i}}{\sqrt{1+\eta^{2}}}\right|z_{i},\mbox{\emph{cigs}}_{i}\right)\\
 & = & \Phi\left(\frac{z_{1i}^{\prime}\delta_{1}+(\gamma_{1}+\beta_{1})\mbox{\emph{cigs}}_{i}}{\sqrt{1+\eta^{2}}}\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

which gives consistent estimates of
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\delta_{1}}{\sqrt{1+\eta^{2}}}
\]

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{(\gamma_{1}+\beta_{1})}{\sqrt{1+\eta^{2}}}
\]

\end_inset

Thus, if 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset

 is correlated with 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{\emph{cigs}}_{i}$
\end_inset

 or is otherwise dependent on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

, then omission of 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset

 is serious.
 In this case, we cannot get consistent estimators of 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma_{1}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part D.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Since 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{\emph{cigs}}_{i}$
\end_inset

 can never be negative and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P(\mbox{\emph{cigs}}_{i}\leq0|z_{i})=0
\]

\end_inset

then it is unable to have a conditional normal distribution in the population.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part E.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Write the model as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
\mbox{\emph{sick}}_{i}^{*} & = & z_{1i}^{\prime}\delta_{1}+\gamma_{1}\mbox{\emph{cigs}}_{i}+q_{i}+\epsilon_{i}\\
\mbox{\emph{cigs}}_{i} & = & z_{i}^{\prime}\beta+v_{i}\\
\mbox{\emph{sick}}_{i} & = & 1\ \left[\mbox{\emph{sick}}_{i}^{*}>0\right]
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\epsilon_{i}\sim N(0,1)$
\end_inset

 and is independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset

 is an unobservable variable possibly correlated with 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{\emph{cigs}}_{i}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{1i}$
\end_inset

 is a subset of 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}\equiv q_{i}+\epsilon_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Equation (3), along with equation (5), is the structural equation; equation
 (4) is a reduced form for 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{\emph{cigs}}_{i}$
\end_inset

, which is endogenous if 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

 are correlated.
 Write the linear project of 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

 as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
u_{i}=\theta v_{i}+e_{i}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta=\frac{\sigma_{uv}}{\sigma_{v}^{2}}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}$
\end_inset

 is independent of 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $e_{i}\sim N(0,\sigma_{e}^{2})$
\end_inset

.
 We can now write
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
\mbox{\emph{sick}}_{i}^{*} & = & z_{1i}^{\prime}\delta_{1}+\gamma_{1}\mbox{\emph{cigs}}_{i}+\theta v_{i}+e_{i}\\
e_{i}|z_{i},\mbox{\emph{cigs}}_{i},v_{i} & \sim & N(0,\sigma_{e}^{2})
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

To test if 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{\emph{cigs}}_{i}$
\end_inset

 is exogenous, consider the null hypothesis 
\begin_inset Formula $H_{0}:\theta=0$
\end_inset

, which implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}=e_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 So, the distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

 plays no role under the null.
 Therefore, the 
\emph on
test
\emph default
 of exogeneity is valid without assuming normality or homoskedasticity of
 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Hence, the test can be applied very broadly, even for 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{\emph{cigs}}_{i}$
\end_inset

, which does not have a conditional normal distribution.
 We test the null by the following procedure:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Obtain the reduced form residuals, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{v}_{i}$
\end_inset

, from regressing 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{\emph{cigs}}_{i}$
\end_inset

 on all exogenous variables.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Run the probit of 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{\emph{sick}}_{i}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{1i}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{\emph{cigs}}_{i}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{v}_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The usual probit 
\begin_inset Formula $t$
\end_inset

 statistic on 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{v}_{i}$
\end_inset

 is a valid test of the null hypothesis that 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{\emph{cigs}}_{i}$
\end_inset

 is exogenous.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part F.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Consider the example given in part B where 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset

 represents the strength of an individual's immune system.
 Suppose that some of the workers live in states that recently implemented
 no-smoking laws in the workplace.
 The presence of the new laws suggests a good IV candidate for 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{\emph{cigs}}$
\end_inset

 because the IV will be correlated with 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{\emph{cigs}}$
\end_inset

, but it will be uncorrelated with 
\begin_inset Formula $q_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 To elaborate, if individual 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 is restricted from smoking in the workplace, then the average amount of
 cigarettes he smokes per day will decrease.
 However, the laws won't have any affect on person 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

's immune system because he can still smoke whenever he is away from work.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Problem 15.12
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Find 
\begin_inset Formula $P(y_{i1}=1,y_{i2}=0,y_{i3}=0|x_{i},c_{i},n_{i}=1)$
\end_inset

 in the fixed effects logit model with 
\begin_inset Formula $T=3$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Solution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

To simply notation, let 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i1}\equiv x_{i1}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i2}\equiv x_{i2}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i3}\equiv x_{i3}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
P(y_{i1}=1,y_{i2}=0,y_{i3}=0|x_{i},c_{i},n_{i}=1) & = & \frac{P(y_{i1}=1,y_{i2}=0,y_{i3}=0,n_{i}=1|x_{i},c_{i})}{P(n_{i}=1|x_{i},c_{i})}\\
 & = & \frac{\mbox{Numerator}}{\mbox{Denominator}}\nonumber \\
\mbox{Numerator} & = & P(y_{i1}=1|x_{i},c_{i})\cdot P(y_{i2}=0|x_{i},c_{i})\cdot P(y_{i3}=0|x_{i},c_{i})\nonumber \\
 & = & \Lambda(z_{i1})\cdot\left[1-\Lambda(z_{i2})\right]\cdot\left[1-\Lambda(z_{i3})\right]\nonumber \\
 & = & \frac{\exp(z_{i1})}{\left[1+\exp(z_{i1})\right]\cdot\left[1+\exp(z_{i2})\right]\cdot\left[1+\exp(z_{i3})\right]}\nonumber \\
\mbox{Denominator} & = & P(y_{i1}=1,y_{i2}=0,y_{i3}=0|x_{i},c_{i})\nonumber \\
 &  & +\ P(y_{i1}=0,y_{i2}=1,y_{i3}=0|x_{i},c_{i})\nonumber \\
 &  & +\ P(y_{i1}=0,y_{i2}=0,y_{i3}=1|x_{i},c_{i})\nonumber 
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

We explicitly write out each probability in the denominator.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
 &  & P(y_{i1}=1,y_{i2}=0,y_{i3}=0|x_{i},c_{i})=\Lambda(z_{i1})\cdot\left[1-\Lambda(z_{i2})\right]\cdot\left[1-\Lambda(z_{i3})\right]\\
 &  & =\frac{\exp(z_{i1})}{\left[1+\exp(z_{i1})\right]\cdot\left[1+\exp(z_{i2})\right]\cdot\left[1+\exp(z_{i3})\right]}\\
 &  & P(y_{i1}=0,y_{i2}=1,y_{i3}=0|x_{i},c_{i})=\left[1-\Lambda(x_{i1}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i})\right]\cdot\Lambda(x_{i2}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i})\cdot\left[1-\Lambda(x_{i3}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i})\right]\\
 &  & =\frac{\exp(z_{i2})}{\left[1+\exp(z_{i1})\right]\cdot\left[1+\exp(z_{i2})\right]\cdot\left[1+\exp(z_{i3})\right]}\\
 &  & P(y_{i1}=0,y_{i2}=0,y_{i3}=1|x_{i},c_{i})=\left[1-\Lambda(x_{i1}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i})\right]\cdot\left[1-\Lambda(x_{i2}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i})\right]\cdot\Lambda(x_{i3}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i})\\
 &  & =\frac{\exp(z_{i3})}{\left[1+\exp(z_{i1})\right]\cdot\left[1+\exp(z_{i2})\right]\cdot\left[1+\exp(z_{i3})\right]}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

So, the denominator is
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\exp(z_{i1})+\exp(z_{i2})+\exp(z_{i3})}{\left[1+\exp(z_{i1})\right]\cdot\left[1+\exp(z_{i2})\right]\cdot\left[1+\exp(z_{i3})\right]}
\]

\end_inset

Thus, equation (6) becomes
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
P(y_{i1}=1,y_{i2}=0,y_{i3}=0|x_{i},c_{i},n_{i}=1)=\frac{\exp(z_{i1})}{\exp(z_{i1})+\exp(z_{i2})+\exp(z_{i3})}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Problem 15.18
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Consider Chamberlain's random effects probit model under assumptions (15.65),
 (15.66), and (15.67), but replace assumption (15.73) with
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
c_{i}|x_{i}\sim N\left[\psi+\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\gamma,\sigma_{a}^{2}\exp(\bar{x}_{i}\lambda)\right]
\]

\end_inset

so that 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{x}_{i}$
\end_inset

 has exponential heteroskedasticity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Find 
\begin_inset Formula $P(y_{it}=1|x_{i},a_{i})$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{i}=c_{i}-E(c_{i}|x_{i})$
\end_inset

.
 Does this probability differ from the probability under assumption (15.73)?
 Explain.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Derive the log-likelihood function by first finding the density of 
\begin_inset Formula $(y_{i1},\ldots,y_{iT})$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Does it have similarities with the log-likelihood funciton under assumption
 (15.73)?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Assuming you have estimated 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\gamma$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{a}^{2}$
\end_inset

, and 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

 by CMLE, how would you estimate the average partial effects? {Hint: First
 show that
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E\left[\Phi(x_{0}^{\prime}\beta+\psi+\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\gamma+a_{i})|x_{i}\right]=\Phi\left(\frac{\left\{ x_{0}^{\prime}\beta+\psi+\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\gamma\right\} }{\sqrt{\left\{ 1+\sigma_{a}^{2}\exp(\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\lambda)\right\} }}\right)
\]

\end_inset

and then use the appropriate average across 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

.}
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Solution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Consider Chamberlain's random effects probit model under the following assumptio
ns:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Strict exogeneity: 
\begin_inset Formula $D(y_{it}|x_{i},c_{i})=D(y_{i}|x_{it},c_{i})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $P(y_{it}=1|x_{it},c_{i})=\Phi(x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Conditional independence: 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i1},\ldots,y_{iT}$
\end_inset

 are independent conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{i},c_{i})$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}|x_{i}\sim N\left[\psi+\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\gamma,\sigma_{a}^{2}\exp(\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\lambda)\right]$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So, 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $\bar{x}_{i}$
\end_inset

 has exponential heteroskedasticity.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part A.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

First off, since 
\begin_inset Formula $E(c_{i}|x_{i})=\psi+\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\gamma$
\end_inset

, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
a_{i}=c_{i}-\psi+\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\gamma
\]

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{i}$
\end_inset

 is a linear combination of 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}$
\end_inset

, which has a normal distribution, and a constant, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
a_{i}|x_{i}\sim N\left[0,\sigma_{a}^{2}\exp(\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\lambda)\right]
\]

\end_inset

Since 
\begin_inset Formula $c_{i}=\psi+\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\gamma+a_{i}$
\end_inset

, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
y_{it}^{*} & = & x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+c_{i}+u_{i}\\
 & = & x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+\psi+\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\gamma+a_{i}+u_{i}\\
 & = & x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+\psi+\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\gamma+v_{i}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $v_{i}\equiv a_{i}+u_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{i}$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 is normally distributed and 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}|(x_{i},a_{i})\sim N(0,1)$
\end_inset

, then 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Cov}(u_{i},a_{i})=0$
\end_inset

, so
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
v_{i}|(x_{i},a_{i})\sim N\left[0,1+\sigma_{a}^{2}\exp(\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\lambda)\right]
\]

\end_inset

Hence
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray}
P(y_{it}=1|x_{i},a_{i}) & = & P(y_{it}|x_{it},a_{i})\nonumber \\
 & = & P(y_{it}^{*}>0|x_{it},a_{i})\\
 & = & P(v_{i}>-x_{it}^{\prime}\beta-\psi-\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\gamma|x_{i},a_{i})\nonumber \\
 & = & P\left(\frac{v_{i}}{\sqrt{1+\sigma_{a}^{2}\exp(\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\gamma})}<\frac{x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+\psi+\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\gamma}{\sqrt{1+\sigma_{a}^{2}\exp(\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\gamma})}\right)\nonumber \\
 & = & \Phi\left(\frac{x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+\psi+\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\gamma}{\sqrt{1+\sigma_{a}^{2}\exp(\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\gamma})}\right)
\end{eqnarray}

\end_inset

Under assumption (15.73), the probability is given by
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
P(y_{it}=1|x_{i},a_{i})=\Phi\left(\frac{x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+\psi+\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\gamma}{\sqrt{1+\sigma_{a}^{2}}}\right)
\end{equation}

\end_inset

This probability can differ from the probability we found in expression
 (9) since
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+\psi+\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\gamma}{\sqrt{1+\sigma_{a}^{2}\exp(\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\lambda)}}\neq\frac{x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+\psi+\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\gamma}{\sqrt{1+\sigma_{a}^{2}}}
\]

\end_inset

when 
\begin_inset Formula $ $
\end_inset


\begin_inset Formula $\exp(\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\lambda)\neq1$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part B.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Under our four given assumptions, a conditional maximum likelihood approach
 is available for estimating 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{a}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Because 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{i}$
\end_inset

 is not observed, it cannot appear in the likelihood function.
 Instead, we find the joint distribution of 
\begin_inset Formula $(y_{i1},\ldots,y_{iT})$
\end_inset

 conditional on 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

, a step that requires us to integrate out 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Since 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{i}|x_{i}\sim N\left[0,\sigma_{a}^{2}\exp(\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\lambda)\right]$
\end_inset

, then
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
f(y_{i1},\ldots,y_{iT}|x_{i};\theta) & = & {\displaystyle \intop_{-\infty}^{\infty}}f(y_{i},a_{i}|x_{i})\ da_{i}\\
 & = & {\displaystyle \intop_{-\infty}^{\infty}}f(y_{i1},\ldots,y_{iT}|x_{i},a_{i})\cdot f(a_{i}|x_{i})\ da_{i}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

By conditional independence and strict exogeneity, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f(y_{i1},\ldots,y_{iT}|x_{i},a_{i})={\displaystyle \prod_{i=1}^{T}}f(y_{it}|x_{i},a_{i})={\displaystyle \prod_{i=1}^{T}}f(y_{it}|x_{it},a_{i})
\]

\end_inset

and from part A,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f(y_{i1},\ldots,y_{iT}|x_{i},a_{i})={\displaystyle \prod_{i=1}^{T}}\Phi(w_{it})^{y_{it}}\left[1-\Phi(w_{it})\right]^{1-y_{it}}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $w_{it}\equiv\frac{x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+\psi+\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\gamma}{\sqrt{1+\sigma_{a}^{2}\exp(\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\gamma})}$
\end_inset

.
 Finally, given the distrubtion of 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{i}$
\end_inset

, we have
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f(a_{i}|x_{i})=\left(\nicefrac{1}{\sqrt{\sigma_{a}^{2}\exp(\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\lambda)}}\right)\phi\left[\nicefrac{a_{i}}{\sqrt{\sigma_{a}^{2}\exp(\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\lambda)}}\right]\equiv h(a,\sigma_{a},\bar{x}_{i},\lambda)
\]

\end_inset

Hence, 
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{equation}
f(y_{i1},\ldots,y_{iT}|x_{i};\theta)={\displaystyle \intop_{-\infty}^{\infty}}\left\{ {\displaystyle \prod_{i=1}^{T}}\Phi(w_{it})^{y_{it}}\left[1-\Phi(w_{it})\right]^{1-y_{it}}\right\} \cdot h(a,\sigma_{a},\bar{x}_{i},\lambda)\ da_{i}
\end{equation}

\end_inset

Taking the log of equation (4) gives the conditional log likelihood 
\begin_inset Formula $l_{i}(\theta)$
\end_inset

 for each 
\begin_inset Formula $i$
\end_inset

 where 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 contains 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{a}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Under assumption (15.73)
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
w_{it}\equiv\frac{x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+\psi+\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\gamma}{\sqrt{1+\sigma_{a}^{2}}}
\]

\end_inset

and
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
h(a,\sigma_{a},\bar{x}_{i},\lambda)\equiv\frac{1}{\sigma_{a}}\phi\left(\frac{a}{\sigma_{a}}\right)
\]

\end_inset

Thus, the log-likelihood functions are almost identical except for the presence
 of 
\begin_inset Formula $\exp(\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\gamma)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part C.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Consider 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
E\left[y_{it}|x_{i},c_{i}\right]=\Phi\left(\frac{x_{it}^{\prime}\beta+\psi+\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\xi}{\sqrt{1+\sigma_{a}^{2}\exp\left(\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\lambda\right)}}\right)
\]

\end_inset

and so, for continuous 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{ij}$
\end_inset

, we have:
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{\partial E\left[y_{it}|x_{i}=x^{o},c_{i}\right]}{\partial x_{j}}=\phi\left(x^{o\prime}\beta_{a}+\psi_{a}+\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\xi_{a}\right)\beta_{a,j}
\]

\end_inset

So,
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
APE=E\left[\phi\left(x^{o\prime}\beta_{a}+\psi_{a}+\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\xi_{a}\right)\beta_{a,j}\right]
\]

\end_inset

where subscript 
\begin_inset Formula $a$
\end_inset

 denotes that the vector has been multiplied by
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left[1+\sigma_{a}^{2}\exp\left(\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\lambda\right)\right]^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}
\]

\end_inset

Since we have estimated 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\xi$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\sigma_{a}^{2}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\lambda$
\end_inset

, we can estimate
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left[1+\sigma_{a}^{2}\exp\left(\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\lambda\right)\right]^{-\nicefrac{1}{2}}
\]

\end_inset

and hence, we can also estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $\beta_{a}$
\end_inset

, 
\begin_inset Formula $\psi_{a}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\xi_{a}$
\end_inset

.
 Now, the APE can be estimated as
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\hat{APE}=N^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\phi\left(x^{o\prime}\hat{\beta}_{a}+\hat{\psi}_{a}+\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\xi}_{a}\right)\hat{\beta}_{a,j}
\]

\end_inset

For a discrete (or even continuous) variable, 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{ik}$
\end_inset

, the 
\begin_inset Formula $APE$
\end_inset

 can be found by evaluating 
\begin_inset Formula $N^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\Phi\left(x^{o\prime}\hat{\beta}_{a}+\hat{\psi}_{a}+\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\xi}_{a}\right)$
\end_inset

 at two different values of 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{o}$
\end_inset

 and taking the difference of the two estimates.
 The two values of 
\begin_inset Formula $x^{o}$
\end_inset

 should be such that 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{ik}$
\end_inset

 changes by one unit and all other variables remain constant i.e.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
x_{1}^{o} & = & \left(x_{1}^{o},\cdots,x_{k-1}^{o},x_{k}^{o},x_{k+1}^{o},\cdots,x_{K}^{o}\right)\\
x_{2}^{o} & = & \left(x_{1}^{o},\cdots,x_{k-1}^{o},x_{k}^{o}+1,x_{k+1}^{o},\cdots,x_{K}^{o}\right)\\
\hat{APE}\left(x_{k}\right) & = & N^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\Phi\left(x_{2}^{o\prime}\hat{\beta}_{a}+\hat{\psi}_{a}+\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\xi}_{a}\right)\\
 & = & -N^{-1}\sum_{i=1}^{N}\Phi\left(x_{1}^{o\prime}\hat{\beta}_{a}+\hat{\psi}_{a}+\bar{x}_{i}^{\prime}\hat{\xi}_{a}\right)
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Problem 17.18 (a and b)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Consider a standard linear model where the endogenous explanatory variable,
 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2}$
\end_inset

, is a corner solution:
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
y_{1} & = & z_{1}\delta_{1}+\alpha_{1}y_{2}+u_{1}\\
E(u_{1}|z) & = & 0
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{1}$
\end_inset

 is a strict subset of 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
What additional assumptions ensure that the 2SLS estimator, using instruments
 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 and a random sample, is consistent for the parameters? Does it matter that
 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2}$
\end_inset

 is a corner solution?
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Suppose you think 
\begin_inset Formula $D(y_{2}|z)$
\end_inset

 follows a Tobit model and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Var}(u_{1}|z)=\sigma_{1}^{2}$
\end_inset

.
 What might you do instead of 2SLS in part A, and why? State a minimal set
 of assumptions that ensure the estimator is consistent.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Solution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part A.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

Let 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 be a 
\begin_inset Formula $L\times1$
\end_inset

 vector of exogenous variables.
 Important to note, the linear projection is always defined when second
 moments of all variables are finite.
 So, the linear projection of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2}$
\end_inset

 onto all the exogenous variables can be represented by the reduced form
 equation
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
L(y_{2i}|z_{i})=z_{i}^{\prime}\beta+v_{i}
\]

\end_inset

where 
\begin_inset Formula $E(v_{i})=0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $E(z_{i}v_{i})=0$
\end_inset

.
 Then, the 2SLS estimators can be obtained from the following steps:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Obtain the fitted values 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{y}_{2}$
\end_inset

 from the OLS regression 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Run the OLS regression of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{y}_{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Consistency of the estimators in the linear projection holds even though
 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}\geq0$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $P(y_{2i}=0)>0$
\end_inset

; nothing about the consistency of OLS hinges on restricting the probabilistic
 features of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2}$
\end_inset

.
 Thus, to ensure that the 2SLS estimator is consistent for the parameters,
 we only need to add the following assumptions: 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{rank}E(z_{i}z_{i}^{\prime})=L$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{rank}E(z_{i}x_{i}^{\prime})=K$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
\bar under
Part B.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset space \quad{}
\end_inset

It is important to note that in part A, we did not make any assumptions
 concerning the conditional mean of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 Instead, to obtain consistent estimators in the first stage of 2SLS, we
 used the linear projection of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2i}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{i}$
\end_inset

, which is the least squares linear predictor.
 However, if we assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $D(y_{2i}|z_{i})$
\end_inset

 follows a Tobit model and 
\begin_inset Formula $\mbox{Var}(u_{1}|z_{i})=\sigma_{1}^{2}$
\end_inset

, then we have made an explicit assumption regarding 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{2i}|z_{i})$
\end_inset

, and it cannot truly be linear.
 As a result, we would like to estimate 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{2i}|z_{i})$
\end_inset

 rather than 
\begin_inset Formula $L(y_{2i}|z_{i})$
\end_inset

 since 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{2i}|z_{i})$
\end_inset

 is the minimum mean square predictor.
 Thus, to obtain consistent estimators of 
\begin_inset Formula $\delta_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\alpha_{1}$
\end_inset

, we follow the following procedure:
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Run a standard type I Tobit of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{2}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $z$
\end_inset

 to obtain 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{y}_{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Enumerate
Run the OLS regression of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{1}$
\end_inset

 on 
\begin_inset Formula $z_{1}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{y}_{2}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
For step 1, write the latent variable model as
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{gather*}
y_{2i}^{*}=z_{i}^{\prime}\beta+v_{i},\ v_{i}|z_{i}\sim N(0,\sigma_{v}^{2})\\
y_{2i}=\max(0,y_{2i}^{*})
\end{gather*}

\end_inset

These assumptions define the type I Tobit model.
 We obtain 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{y}_{2i}$
\end_inset

 by evaluating 
\begin_inset Formula $E(y_{2i}|z_{i})$
\end_inset

 at 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 and the MLEs, 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\beta}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{\sigma}_{v}$
\end_inset

.
 Let 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}_{1}$
\end_inset

 be defined as 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}_{1}=\left(z_{1},\hat{y}_{2}\right)$
\end_inset

.
 So, for step 2, we need 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{x}_{1}\hat{x}_{1}^{\prime}$
\end_inset

 to have full column rank.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Section*
Probelm 19.6
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let 
\begin_inset Formula $f(\cdot|x_{i};\theta)$
\end_inset

 denote the density of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

 for a random draw from the population.
 Find the conditional density of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{i},s_{i}=1)$
\end_inset

 when the selection rule is 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{i}=1\left[a_{1}(x_{i})<y_{i}<a_{2}(x_{i})\right]$
\end_inset

, where 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{1}(x_{i})$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{2}(x_{i})$
\end_inset

 are known functions of 
\begin_inset Formula $x_{i}$
\end_inset

.
 In the Hausman and Wise (1977) example, 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{2}(x_{i})$
\end_inset

 was function of family size because the poverty income level depends on
 family size.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Subsection*
Solution
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Assume 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}=x_{i}^{\prime}\beta+u_{i}$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $u_{i}|x_{i}\sim N(0,\sigma_{u}^{2})$
\end_inset

.
 This implies that 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}|x_{i}\sim N(x_{i}^{\prime}\beta,\sigma_{u}^{2})$
\end_inset

.
 We wish to find the conditional density of 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

 given 
\begin_inset Formula $(x_{i},s_{i}=1)$
\end_inset

 when the selection rule is 
\begin_inset Formula $s_{i}=1\left[a_{1}(x_{i})<y_{i}<a_{2}(x_{i})\right]$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Formula 
\begin{eqnarray*}
F(y_{i}|x_{i},s_{i}=1) & = & P(Y\leq y_{i}|x_{i},s_{i}=1)\\
 & = & \frac{P(Y\leq y_{i},s_{i}=1|x_{i})}{P(s_{i}=1|x_{i})}\\
 & = & \frac{\Phi\left(\frac{y_{i}-x_{i}^{\prime}\beta}{\sigma_{u}}\right)-\Phi\left(\frac{a_{1}(x_{i})-x_{i}^{\prime}\beta}{\sigma}\right)}{\Phi\left(\frac{a_{2}(x_{i})-x_{i}^{\prime}\beta}{\sigma}\right)-\Phi\left(\frac{a_{1}(x_{i})-x_{i}^{\prime}\beta}{\sigma}\right)}
\end{eqnarray*}

\end_inset

Thus, to obtain 
\begin_inset Formula $f(y_{i}|x_{i},s_{i}=1;\theta)$
\end_inset

, we take the derivative of 
\begin_inset Formula $F(y_{i}|x_{i},s_{i}=1)$
\end_inset

 with respect to 
\begin_inset Formula $y_{i}$
\end_inset

.
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
f(y_{i}|x_{i},s_{i}=1;\theta)=\frac{\partial F(y_{i}|x_{i},s_{i}=1)}{\partial y_{i}}=\frac{1}{\sigma_{u}}\frac{\phi\left(\frac{y_{i}-x_{i}^{\prime}\beta}{\sigma_{u}}\right)}{\Phi\left(\frac{a_{2}(x_{i})-x_{i}^{\prime}\beta}{\sigma}\right)-\Phi\left(\frac{a_{1}(x_{i})-x_{i}^{\prime}\beta}{\sigma}\right)}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

